<h1>What Is SHA-256 and Why Is It Used So Often?</h1>
<p>SHA-256 is one of the most common hash algorithms in current software, security, and file verification workflows. It belongs to the SHA-2 family and produces a 256-bit hash value. Like MD5, it turns input data into a fixed-length digest. Unlike MD5, it remains a widely accepted choice for many modern integrity and cryptographic uses.</p>
<p>NIST defines SHA-256 in the Secure Hash Standard, <a href="https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/180-4/upd1/final">FIPS 180-4</a>. The standard covers several secure hash algorithms, including SHA-224, SHA-256, SHA-384, and SHA-512. The names generally describe the digest size. SHA-256 outputs 256 bits, usually shown as 64 hexadecimal characters.</p>
<p>The most common everyday use is verification. A software publisher can publish the SHA-256 checksum of an installer. After downloading the file, you calculate its SHA-256 hash locally and compare the two values. If they match exactly, the file matches the publisher's listed digest. If not, the file may be corrupted, incomplete, or different from what was intended.</p>
<p>SHA-256 is also used in many developer workflows. APIs may use SHA-256 inside signing processes. Build systems may use it to identify artifacts. Package managers may use it to verify downloaded dependencies. Backup tools may use it to detect changed files. In those situations, the hash is not meant to hide the data. It is meant to identify or verify it.</p>
<p>SHA-256 should not be confused with password hashing. It is stronger than MD5 for general hashing, but it is still fast. Password storage needs algorithms that intentionally cost time and sometimes memory for each guess. That is why password systems usually rely on bcrypt, Argon2id, scrypt, or PBKDF2 instead of plain SHA-256.</p>
<p>Compared with MD5, SHA-256 is a better default for new file checksums. MD5 may still appear in legacy documentation, as covered in <a href="/md5-vs-sha256.html">MD5 vs SHA-256</a>, but SHA-256 gives a stronger margin for modern use. It is supported in Python hashlib, Node.js crypto, OpenSSL, PowerShell, Linux checksum utilities, and many other tools.</p>
<p>There is a practical cost: SHA-256 output is longer than MD5 output. That can make it less convenient to read by eye. But the difference is not a serious problem in normal workflows. Users should copy and compare the full value rather than visually scanning a few characters. A longer hash is still easy to store, publish, and automate.</p>
<p>A practical benefit of SHA-256 is that it travels well between systems. The same file should produce the same SHA-256 value on Windows, macOS, Linux, Python, Node.js, PHP, OpenSSL, and many package tools. That makes SHA-256 useful for documentation, release notes, build pipelines, and long-term archives where different tools may be used over time.</p>
<p>For beginners, the safest mental model is to treat SHA-256 as a file and data fingerprint, not as a way to hide information. Anyone with the same input can calculate the same value. That repeatability is the point. It makes comparison possible without sending the whole file again.</p>
<p>The best way to use SHA-256 is to make it your standard checksum choice unless a system specifically requires something else. For downloads, backups, data exports, and release files, it is widely supported and easy to verify. MD5 is worth understanding because old systems still use it. SHA-256 is the better habit for new work.</p>
